  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the kitchen, Kostya tells Lyova about a problem with a customer's dessert. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я никому не нужен.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Люба, клиент нашел сим-карту в десерте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сим-карту? В десерте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, он требует шеф-повара.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Иди к нему, Лёва.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не пойду туда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что значит, ты не пойдешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты сегодня шеф-повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Иди и разбирайся с этим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я не шеф-повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Лёва, тебе надо разобраться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я боюсь туда идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Давай, иди.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around SIM Card in Dessert line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я никому не нужен.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not needed by anyone.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'I' straight to 'is needed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Люба, клиент нашел сим-карту в десерте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyuba, a customer found a SIM card in the dessert.' Here Kostya anchors the line on the word meaning 'client' and builds the thought around 'found' and 'SIM card'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сим-карту? В десерте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A SIM card? In the dessert?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'dessert' is enough to make it a question. And Kostya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, он требует шеф-повара.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, he demands the chef.' Kostya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'demands' for the ongoing action, followed by 'head chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Иди к нему, Лёва.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go to him, Lyova.' Kostya uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не пойду туда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will not go there.' Lyova uses the perfective verb meaning 'go' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'there'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что значит, ты не пойдешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you mean, you will not go?' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'will go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты сегодня шеф-повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are the chef today.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'today' directly with 'head chef', combining 'today' with 'head chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Иди и разбирайся с этим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go and figure this out.' Kostya leads with the direct command form meaning 'go', followed by 'to understand well'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я не шеф-повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I am not the chef.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'but' directly with 'head chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Лёва, тебе надо разобраться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lyova, you need to figure it out.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to deal with', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Lyova responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я боюсь туда идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am afraid to go there.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'am afraid' turns the action back onto the subject. And Kostya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Давай, иди.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, go.' Kostya leads with the direct command form meaning 'let's', followed by 'go'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
